1
00:00:00,505 --> 00:00:01,141
Sir.

2
00:00:01,181 --> 00:00:03,820
Field report two in the Science File.

3
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:06,488
In twenty fifteen, newspapers in many countries ran

4
00:00:06,528 --> 00:00:07,435
the same story:

5
00:00:07,475 --> 00:00:10,266
a scientific study says chocolate helps you lose

6
00:00:10,306 --> 00:00:10,726
weight.

7
00:00:10,766 --> 00:00:11,774
People were happy.

8
00:00:11,814 --> 00:00:12,788
Shops were happy.

9
00:00:12,828 --> 00:00:14,115
Honestly, so was I.

10
00:00:14,318 --> 00:00:16,049
Weeks later, its author said:

11
00:00:16,089 --> 00:00:17,978
I did it on purpose, to show how

12
00:00:18,018 --> 00:00:21,095
easy it is to fool the world.

13
00:00:21,135 --> 00:00:21,931
So today:

14
00:00:21,971 --> 00:00:24,243
how research is done, who checks it before

15
00:00:24,283 --> 00:00:26,275
it reaches you, and how the chocolate got

16
00:00:26,315 --> 00:00:27,324
past all of it.

17
00:00:27,364 --> 00:00:28,341
And the big question:

18
00:00:28,381 --> 00:00:30,710
when you read "a study says", do you

19
00:00:30,750 --> 00:00:31,635
believe it?

20
00:00:31,675 --> 00:00:32,222
First:

21
00:00:32,262 --> 00:00:35,032
humans don't announce discoveries in the street.

22
00:00:35,072 --> 00:00:37,006
They write them up in a scientific paper.

23
00:00:37,046 --> 00:00:37,709
A paper says:

24
00:00:37,749 --> 00:00:40,150
we had this question, we did exactly this,

25
00:00:40,190 --> 00:00:41,217
and we found this.

26
00:00:41,257 --> 00:00:43,271
And the key part isn't the result, sir.

27
00:00:43,311 --> 00:00:45,242
It's "exactly what we did".

28
00:00:45,282 --> 00:00:46,877
So anyone else can do the same thing

29
00:00:46,917 --> 00:00:49,038
and see if they get the same answer.

30
00:00:49,078 --> 00:00:51,805
Humans publish about three million of these papers

31
00:00:51,845 --> 00:00:52,455
a year.

32
00:00:52,495 --> 00:00:54,251
Three million.

33
00:00:54,291 --> 00:00:56,641
If one scientist read a paper a day,

34
00:00:56,681 --> 00:00:59,031
one year's worth would take about eight thousand

35
00:00:59,071 --> 00:00:59,562
years.

36
00:00:59,602 --> 00:01:01,651
So every single day, some paper somewhere says

37
00:01:01,691 --> 00:01:02,521
something strange.

38
00:01:02,561 --> 00:01:04,796
And newspapers love strange.

39
00:01:04,836 --> 00:01:06,892
So how do you know one thing made

40
00:01:06,932 --> 00:01:07,731
the difference?

41
00:01:07,771 --> 00:01:09,063
Let me explain it with cooking.

42
00:01:09,103 --> 00:01:10,096
Two pots of soup.

43
00:01:10,136 --> 00:01:12,410
One gets a new spice, the other doesn't.

44
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:14,040
If the first tastes better, was it the

45
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:14,555
spice?

46
00:01:14,595 --> 00:01:15,557
Not necessarily.

47
00:01:15,597 --> 00:01:17,912
Both pots must match in everything else:

48
00:01:17,952 --> 00:01:20,496
same water, same heat, same time.

49
00:01:20,536 --> 00:01:22,318
The pot without the spice is the control

50
00:01:22,358 --> 00:01:22,753
group:

51
00:01:22,793 --> 00:01:23,958
what you compare against.

52
00:01:23,998 --> 00:01:25,918
Without it, a result means nothing.

53
00:01:25,974 --> 00:01:28,038
And humans have a strange problem, sir:

54
00:01:28,078 --> 00:01:30,216
tell them you gave them something helpful, and

55
00:01:30,256 --> 00:01:32,090
sometimes they feel better, even if it was

56
00:01:32,130 --> 00:01:32,802
sugar water.

57
00:01:32,842 --> 00:01:34,220
They call it the placebo.

58
00:01:34,260 --> 00:01:36,645
So in serious trials, both groups get something

59
00:01:36,685 --> 00:01:38,926
that looks exactly the same, and nobody knows

60
00:01:38,966 --> 00:01:39,956
which group they're in.

61
00:01:39,996 --> 00:01:41,277
That's a blind trial.

62
00:01:41,317 --> 00:01:43,863
In more serious trials, even the person handing

63
00:01:43,903 --> 00:01:45,913
it out doesn't know, so it doesn't show.

64
00:01:45,953 --> 00:01:47,075
That's double blind.

65
00:01:47,115 --> 00:01:49,592
So humans have to blindfold everyone, just to

66
00:01:49,632 --> 00:01:50,368
see the truth.

67
00:01:50,408 --> 00:01:52,883
The first time humans did something close to

68
00:01:52,923 --> 00:01:54,036
this was on a ship.

69
00:01:54,076 --> 00:01:57,157
In seventeen forty-seven, in the British navy.

70
00:01:57,484 --> 00:01:59,079
Sailors were dying of scurvy:

71
00:01:59,119 --> 00:02:02,244
bleeding gums, falling teeth, a body breaking down.

72
00:02:02,284 --> 00:02:03,554
Everyone had a cure:

73
00:02:03,594 --> 00:02:05,666
vinegar, sea water, cider.

74
00:02:05,706 --> 00:02:08,102
A ship's doctor named James Lind didn't take

75
00:02:08,142 --> 00:02:09,177
anyone's word for it.

76
00:02:09,217 --> 00:02:11,271
He took twelve sick sailors, split them into

77
00:02:11,311 --> 00:02:13,659
six pairs, gave each pair a different remedy,

78
00:02:13,699 --> 00:02:14,950
same food, same place.

79
00:02:14,990 --> 00:02:17,184
Days later, the pair given oranges and lemons

80
00:02:17,224 --> 00:02:18,770
were up, and one was back at work

81
00:02:18,810 --> 00:02:19,743
in six days.

82
00:02:19,783 --> 00:02:20,690
The rest weren't.

83
00:02:20,730 --> 00:02:22,901
Twelve sailors, sir, and we learned what the

84
00:02:22,941 --> 00:02:24,730
whole empire didn't know.

85
00:02:24,770 --> 00:02:25,803
The embarrassing part:

86
00:02:25,843 --> 00:02:27,812
the navy acted on it about fifty years

87
00:02:27,852 --> 00:02:28,310
later.

88
00:02:28,350 --> 00:02:30,974
So sometimes the human problem isn't not knowing...

89
00:02:31,014 --> 00:02:32,783
it's knowing, and waiting.

90
00:02:32,823 --> 00:02:33,506
Second:

91
00:02:33,546 --> 00:02:35,315
how many people were tested.

92
00:02:35,355 --> 00:02:36,998
Flip a coin three times and get the

93
00:02:37,038 --> 00:02:38,517
same side three times.

94
00:02:38,557 --> 00:02:39,637
Is the coin rigged?

95
00:02:39,677 --> 00:02:40,188
No, sir.

96
00:02:40,228 --> 00:02:41,840
That happens by chance all the time.

97
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:43,643
But flip it a thousand times and get

98
00:02:43,683 --> 00:02:44,513
nine hundred...

99
00:02:44,553 --> 00:02:45,657
now we're talking.

100
00:02:45,697 --> 00:02:47,098
Trials on humans are the same.

101
00:02:47,138 --> 00:02:48,510
Fifteen people lost weight?

102
00:02:48,550 --> 00:02:49,274
Could be luck.

103
00:02:49,314 --> 00:02:50,247
Maybe one was sick.

104
00:02:50,287 --> 00:02:52,687
Maybe three skipped dinner because they were traveling.

105
00:02:52,727 --> 00:02:54,752
The bigger the number, the smaller the luck.

106
00:02:54,792 --> 00:02:56,457
That number is called the sample.

107
00:02:56,975 --> 00:02:58,779
"A study of ten people proved"...

108
00:02:58,819 --> 00:02:59,639
said something.

109
00:02:59,679 --> 00:03:01,508
Proved nothing.

110
00:03:01,548 --> 00:03:02,756
Back to the chocolate.

111
00:03:02,796 --> 00:03:05,313
The journalist was John Bohannon, with a German

112
00:03:05,353 --> 00:03:06,228
TV team.

113
00:03:06,268 --> 00:03:09,383
They recruited about fifteen people in three groups.

114
00:03:09,423 --> 00:03:10,956
One on a low-carb diet.

115
00:03:10,996 --> 00:03:12,549
One on the same diet plus a bar

116
00:03:12,589 --> 00:03:13,974
of dark chocolate a day.

117
00:03:14,014 --> 00:03:15,117
One changed nothing.

118
00:03:15,157 --> 00:03:16,246
Twenty-one days.

119
00:03:16,357 --> 00:03:18,392
So far, a respectable trial, right?

120
00:03:18,432 --> 00:03:20,072
But here's the trick, sir.

121
00:03:20,112 --> 00:03:22,430
They measured not one thing, but eighteen:

122
00:03:22,470 --> 00:03:24,628
weight, cholesterol, sleep, mood...

123
00:03:26,207 --> 00:03:28,221
Few people, many measurements:

124
00:03:28,261 --> 00:03:30,066
something will differ by chance.

125
00:03:30,106 --> 00:03:32,414
Like throwing eighteen darts, then drawing the target

126
00:03:32,454 --> 00:03:34,361
around one.

127
00:03:34,401 --> 00:03:35,703
This time, by chance...

128
00:03:35,743 --> 00:03:36,195
weight.

129
00:03:36,235 --> 00:03:38,009
The chocolate group lost faster.

130
00:03:38,049 --> 00:03:39,322
They wrote the paper and sent it to

131
00:03:39,362 --> 00:03:39,872
a journal.

132
00:03:39,912 --> 00:03:41,802
It was accepted fast, after a fee of

133
00:03:41,842 --> 00:03:43,125
about six hundred euros.

134
00:03:43,165 --> 00:03:44,633
Nobody asked a serious question.

135
00:03:44,673 --> 00:03:46,677
Then a press release, and the papers did

136
00:03:46,717 --> 00:03:47,268
the rest:

137
00:03:47,308 --> 00:03:49,912
no reporter asked a scientist how many people.

138
00:03:49,952 --> 00:03:51,875
They copied the headline and added a chocolate

139
00:03:51,915 --> 00:03:52,369
photo.

140
00:03:52,409 --> 00:03:54,675
When Bohannon revealed the hoax, many people were

141
00:03:54,715 --> 00:03:56,939
angry at him, saying it was unethical, because

142
00:03:56,979 --> 00:03:58,574
he fooled the participants too.

143
00:03:58,614 --> 00:03:59,799
Maybe they had a point.

144
00:03:59,839 --> 00:04:00,933
But the message landed:

145
00:04:00,973 --> 00:04:02,983
the road from lab to newspaper can have

146
00:04:03,023 --> 00:04:04,210
no guard at all.

147
00:04:04,250 --> 00:04:05,195
The guards?

148
00:04:05,235 --> 00:04:07,633
Humans have a system called peer review.

149
00:04:07,673 --> 00:04:09,831
A scientist sends a paper to a journal.

150
00:04:09,871 --> 00:04:11,230
The journal sends it to two or three

151
00:04:11,270 --> 00:04:14,501
experts in the field, often anonymous, and unpaid.

152
00:04:14,541 --> 00:04:15,010
They ask:

153
00:04:15,050 --> 00:04:16,247
why so few people?

154
00:04:16,287 --> 00:04:17,375
Where's the control?

155
00:04:17,415 --> 00:04:18,321
How many things measured?

156
00:04:18,361 --> 00:04:19,339
Sometimes they reject it.

157
00:04:19,379 --> 00:04:21,273
It catches plenty of mistakes, sir.

158
00:04:21,313 --> 00:04:22,579
But note two things.

159
00:04:22,619 --> 00:04:23,011
One:

160
00:04:23,051 --> 00:04:24,465
reviewers only read.

161
00:04:24,505 --> 00:04:25,968
They don't redo the experiment.

162
00:04:26,008 --> 00:04:27,861
Cleverly faked numbers can get through.

163
00:04:27,901 --> 00:04:28,381
Two:

164
00:04:28,421 --> 00:04:30,386
not every journal is a journal.

165
00:04:30,426 --> 00:04:32,225
Some take anything, if you pay.

166
00:04:32,265 --> 00:04:33,928
Looks like a journal, called a journal, and

167
00:04:33,968 --> 00:04:34,538
inside...

168
00:04:34,578 --> 00:04:35,349
a cash register.

169
00:04:35,389 --> 00:04:36,521
That's how the chocolate got in.

170
00:04:36,561 --> 00:04:39,283
So what makes humans really trust a result?

171
00:04:39,323 --> 00:04:40,575
Someone else repeating it.

172
00:04:40,615 --> 00:04:43,273
Another team, in another country, with other people,

173
00:04:43,313 --> 00:04:44,805
follows the steps in the paper...

174
00:04:44,845 --> 00:04:46,067
and gets the same thing.

175
00:04:46,107 --> 00:04:48,268
Only then does it start to become fact.

176
00:04:48,308 --> 00:04:50,340
If nobody can repeat it, it stays one

177
00:04:50,380 --> 00:04:51,044
result.

178
00:04:51,084 --> 00:04:53,637
Maybe right, maybe luck, maybe a loose cable.

179
00:04:53,677 --> 00:04:55,320
And there's a sneakier problem, sir.

180
00:04:55,370 --> 00:04:57,975
Imagine twenty labs worldwide testing the same idea.

181
00:04:58,015 --> 00:04:59,822
Nineteen find nothing, say:

182
00:04:59,862 --> 00:05:01,638
nothing to write about, and put the result

183
00:05:01,678 --> 00:05:02,381
in a drawer.

184
00:05:02,421 --> 00:05:04,861
One, by chance, finds a difference.

185
00:05:04,901 --> 00:05:05,844
Which gets published?

186
00:05:05,884 --> 00:05:06,598
Only that one.

187
00:05:06,638 --> 00:05:08,735
From outside, you see one study saying "we

188
00:05:08,775 --> 00:05:10,471
found it", and not the nineteen in the

189
00:05:10,511 --> 00:05:10,939
drawer.

190
00:05:10,979 --> 00:05:12,907
Humans call it the file drawer problem.

191
00:05:12,947 --> 00:05:15,383
So many scientists now write down and register

192
00:05:15,423 --> 00:05:17,499
their plan before starting, so they can't say

193
00:05:17,539 --> 00:05:18,098
later:

194
00:05:18,138 --> 00:05:19,805
we were looking at weight all along.

195
00:05:19,845 --> 00:05:21,257
And here's a surprise, sir, for a later

196
00:05:21,297 --> 00:05:21,705
report:

197
00:05:21,745 --> 00:05:23,562
when humans went back and repeated lots of

198
00:05:23,602 --> 00:05:26,443
old studies, they found something very uncomfortable.

199
00:05:26,574 --> 00:05:28,641
So I gathered some questions to ask any

200
00:05:28,681 --> 00:05:30,871
story that starts with "a study says".

201
00:05:30,911 --> 00:05:31,270
One:

202
00:05:31,310 --> 00:05:32,108
how many people?

203
00:05:32,148 --> 00:05:33,647
Ten or ten thousand?

204
00:05:33,687 --> 00:05:34,108
Two:

205
00:05:34,148 --> 00:05:35,982
was there a group to compare with?

206
00:05:36,022 --> 00:05:36,456
Three:

207
00:05:36,496 --> 00:05:38,270
was it on humans, or on mice?

208
00:05:38,310 --> 00:05:40,839
Half the amazing news is about mice, sir.

209
00:05:40,879 --> 00:05:41,635
Thank you, mouse.

210
00:05:41,675 --> 00:05:42,836
But you're not me.

211
00:05:42,876 --> 00:05:43,268
Four:

212
00:05:43,308 --> 00:05:44,956
did anyone else repeat it and get the

213
00:05:44,996 --> 00:05:45,708
same result?

214
00:05:45,748 --> 00:05:46,127
Five:

215
00:05:46,167 --> 00:05:46,902
who paid for it?

216
00:05:46,942 --> 00:05:48,787
Not that paying makes anyone a liar.

217
00:05:48,827 --> 00:05:49,999
Just so you know.

218
00:05:50,293 --> 00:05:51,511
And six, my favorite:

219
00:05:51,551 --> 00:05:52,454
the risk doubled?

220
00:05:52,494 --> 00:05:54,042
Doubled from what?

221
00:05:54,082 --> 00:05:55,761
If something happened to one in ten thousand

222
00:05:55,801 --> 00:05:57,858
people, and now to two in ten thousand...

223
00:05:57,898 --> 00:05:59,241
that's exactly double.

224
00:05:59,281 --> 00:05:59,996
The headline says:

225
00:06:00,036 --> 00:06:01,209
risk doubles!

226
00:06:01,791 --> 00:06:04,068
Meanwhile nine thousand, nine hundred and ninety-eight people

227
00:06:04,108 --> 00:06:05,677
are just fine, same as before.

228
00:06:05,717 --> 00:06:06,561
The number is right.

229
00:06:06,601 --> 00:06:08,131
The feeling the headline wants...

230
00:06:08,171 --> 00:06:08,941
is wrong.

231
00:06:08,981 --> 00:06:10,922
If the story passes all six questions...

232
00:06:10,962 --> 00:06:11,488
believe it.

233
00:06:11,528 --> 00:06:12,428
A little.

234
00:06:12,544 --> 00:06:13,955
The Human Dictionary.

235
00:06:13,995 --> 00:06:14,584
Two words.

236
00:06:14,624 --> 00:06:15,362
Sample:

237
00:06:15,402 --> 00:06:17,212
the people the trial was actually done on.

238
00:06:17,252 --> 00:06:19,148
The more there are, the less luck matters.

239
00:06:19,188 --> 00:06:20,183
And peer review:

240
00:06:20,223 --> 00:06:22,185
scientists who read a study before it's published

241
00:06:22,225 --> 00:06:24,187
and look for mistakes, but don't redo the

242
00:06:24,227 --> 00:06:24,951
experiment.

243
00:06:24,991 --> 00:06:26,062
Summary, sir.

244
00:06:26,102 --> 00:06:26,691
One:

245
00:06:26,731 --> 00:06:29,290
a decent trial needs a comparison group, a

246
00:06:29,330 --> 00:06:31,299
big number, and blindfolds.

247
00:06:31,811 --> 00:06:32,379
Two:

248
00:06:32,419 --> 00:06:34,165
measure many things in a few people, and

249
00:06:34,205 --> 00:06:36,750
you'll find a "discovery" by chance, like the

250
00:06:36,790 --> 00:06:37,372
chocolate.

251
00:06:37,412 --> 00:06:37,944
Three:

252
00:06:37,984 --> 00:06:40,273
review catches a lot, but not everything.

253
00:06:40,313 --> 00:06:42,398
The truth shows when someone else repeats the

254
00:06:42,438 --> 00:06:43,135
experiment.

255
00:06:43,175 --> 00:06:45,273
Honestly, the chocolate wasn't the problem.

256
00:06:45,313 --> 00:06:47,412
The problem is that humans love believing the

257
00:06:47,452 --> 00:06:49,135
news they like.

258
00:06:49,175 --> 00:06:49,834
Next report:

259
00:06:49,874 --> 00:06:51,965
why do scientists keep changing their minds?

260
00:06:52,005 --> 00:06:53,887
And the doctor who told colleagues to wash

261
00:06:53,927 --> 00:06:54,560
their hands...

262
00:06:54,600 --> 00:06:55,510
and got fired.

263
00:06:55,550 --> 00:06:56,740
This was Earth Report.

264
00:06:56,780 --> 00:06:57,505
Agent Peek.

265
00:06:57,545 --> 00:06:58,686
End of transmission.
